  "observations": "Part A. `AM_ONPOLICY_LAYERS` -> `onpolicy_layers` (group size N; 0 = off) plus a second, untested axis `AM_ONPOLICY_DOCKV` -> `onpolicy_refresh_doc_kv`. Group size 4 was chosen: 36 layers = 9 groups, 8 re-extractions per document, before layers 4,8,...,32; per-layer would be 35. Unit sanity (CPU and CUDA agree): the OFF arm is bit-identical to a `git archive HEAD` snapshot run in a separate interpreter; passing the hook with the knob off is bit-identical (the hook is never called); and with the knob ON but a refresh that returns the SAME queries, every number (mean_mse, per-layer MSE, k/v sums, |v|max, mass_on_S, n_queries) is bit-identical to OFF -- so the restructuring changes only the queries, not the math. Six corruption modes (wrong shape, empty layer, wrong batch count, non-finite, non-tuple return, bad doc-KV shape) and 'knob on with no hook' all raise. The `onpolicy`/`onpolicy_events` keys appear in `am_doc_*.pt` only when the mechanism ran.\nPart B, 3 arms, theta=5e6, top32, beta off, 0 gradient steps. CONTROL REPRODUCED THE GATE EXACTLY: in-run QA 2.15320086479187 / MT 2.5296061038970947 and standalone QA 2.159724712371826 / MT 2.529625177383423 -- all four to the last printed digit, |v|max 178.0, mean am/mean_mse 0.014059890443260059, identical to MECH-KEYS' control.\nTHE MECHANISM IS LIVE AND THE ACTIVATION SHIFT IS SMALL. Every document ran 8 refreshes; the per-group query drift (stale vs on-policy queries at the boundary layer) is mean cosine 0.99817 and mean relative L2 4.98% (max 18.9% on a single boundary), and it does NOT widen with depth: rel-L2 by boundary layer is 9.20/2.55/3.61/7.95/6.57/5.44/2.47/2.05% at L4..L32, i.e. largest at the SHALLOWEST boundary. LIT-006 predicted the gap should widen with layer index; it does not.\nON-POLICY ALONE (frozen keys): QA 2.15972 -> 2.13984 (-0.0199), MT 2.52963 -> 2.50850 (-0.0211). Both are ~1/5 of the 0.1-0.2 noise band. COMPOSED WITH MECH-005 (the best gradient-free point): QA 2.09319 / MT 2.38128 versus MECH-005's own QA 2.03492 / MT 2.33050 -- i.e. +0.0583 QA and +0.0508 MT WORSE, inside the band but on the wrong side. MT does not move materially below 2.33; the bar (MT <= 2.02) is still 0.36 away.\nTHE ROUTING COLLAPSE THIS ENTRY WAS OPENED FOR IS ALREADY GONE, SO THERE WAS NOTHING TO REPAIR. Total eval-time cartridge attention mass on MT: control 0.5930, on-policy 0.5918 (-0.0012), on-policy+keys 0.6714, against the untouched Phase-1 cartridge's 0.5879. The 0.588 -> 0.329 collapse recorded by ORACLE-WRITE was a theta=1e4 measurement; at the model's true rotary base (MECH-003) the control already sits at Phase-1 level. On-policy therefore neither prevents nor improves it.\nSelectivity is untouched: the MT/QA mass_on_S ratio is 1.0428 (control) -> 1.0413 (on-policy) -> 1.0554 (on-policy+keys), all below Phase-1's 1.0812, and eval-time mass_on_S on MT is 0.0711 -> 0.0720 with frozen keys. The solve's own objective got slightly WORSE with on-policy queries (mean am/mean_mse 0.014060 -> 0.014377 frozen-key; 0.003940 -> 0.004428 in the keys arm), and |v|max moved 178.0 -> 169.0 / 74.0 -> 75.5.\nCOST (a success axis): 8 extra reference-query passes per document cost 342-353 s over 16 documents (~21-22 s/doc, ~2.6-2.9 s per refresh). solve_s 164.8 s -> 586.2 s (3.56x) frozen-key and -> 726.4 s (4.41x control, 2.67x MECH-005's 272 s). phase2_e2e_s 593 / 630 / 1195 s (the control's e2e is inflated by a ~6 min nv_kthread_q_flush exit hang). gradient_steps = 0 for all arms.\nK-CURVE (k in {8,12,16}, standalone, both splits): on-policy frozen-key MT 2.4733 / 2.4938 / 2.5085 -- monotonically WORSE in k, minimum at the earliest measured point; on-policy+keys MT 2.3247 / 2.4483 / 2.3813 -- non-monotone with its minimum at k=8. Neither reproduces the value-only shape (bottom at k=12 then degrade); on-policy does not remove the late-document degradation and in the frozen-key arm moves the best k earlier. Only three k were measured, so the shape statement is coarse. QA at k=8 is 1.9547 / 1.9479, far below the Phase-1 floor 2.2388.\nCAVEATS: one seed, no seed variation (there is no SEED knob, DIAG-KEYCURVE seed_probe). Slot selection is deliberately held fixed at the pre-write ranking so the only variable is the queries. The doc-KV refresh axis (`AM_ONPOLICY_DOCKV`) is implemented and unit-tested but was NOT run on GPU. `cartridges.am.*` uses a bare `logging.getLogger`, so the per-refresh INFO lines do not reach the run log; the refresh record lives in `am_doc_*.pt` `extra['onpolicy']`. Every number here is from a wandb-logged run.\nMATCHED-k COMPARISON (added after DIAG-KEYCURVE published MECH-005's own k-curve; same harness, same splits): at every k measured, adding on-policy to MECH-005 is equal or worse. MT at k=8 2.3289 -> 2.3247 (-0.004), k=12 **2.2720 -> 2.4483 (+0.176)**, k=16 2.3305 -> 2.3813 (+0.051); QA at k=8 1.9464 -> 1.9479 (+0.001), k=12 **1.9560 -> 2.1440 (+0.188)**, k=16 2.0349 -> 2.0932 (+0.058). At k=12 -- the k where MECH-005 is actually best -- on-policy costs +0.176 MT and +0.188 QA, both AT OR ABOVE the top of the 0.1-0.2 noise band. This is the only on-policy-vs-no-on-policy delta in the whole experiment that escapes the noise floor, and it points the wrong way.",
